  <model metaid="meta_dapagliflozin_liver" id="dapagliflozin_liver" name="Model for the hepatic metabolism of dapagliflozin." substanceUnits="mmole" timeUnits="min" volumeUnits="litre" areaUnits="m2" lengthUnits="metre" extentUnits="mmole">
    <notes>
      <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <p>Model for the hepatic metabolism of dapagliflozin.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Dapagliflozin is metabolized mainly by UGT1A9 to the inactive
dapagliflozin-3-O-glucuronide (D3G, 61 % of an oral dose in the urine),
and to a minor extent to the 2-O-glucuronide (UGT2B4/2B7) and oxidative
metabolites (CYP3A4), about 13 % of the dose in the urine and 6 % in the
feces [Obermeier2010]. Shares of the metabolism: UGT1A9 79.9 %, CYP3A4
9.8 %, UGT2B4/2B7 8.9 % [Jo2021].</li>
          <li>The metabolism is a first-order clearance of the plasma of the liver:
the pharmacokinetics are dose proportional from 0.1 to 500 mg
[Komoroski2009], far below the Km of UGT1A9 (479 µM in kidney microsomes
[Kasichayanula2013]). It scales with the volume of the liver tissue, so
with the tissue loss in cirrhosis.</li>
          <li>The fraction <code>f_d3g</code> of the metabolism forms D3G, the rest the other
metabolites (<code>dapmet</code>).</li>
          <li>The exposure rises with the Child-Pugh class (AUC ratio 1.03, 1.36, 1.67
[Kasichayanula2011]).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The content of this model has been carefully created in a manual research effort.</p>
